 "incumbency": -2.0, "money": 0.0}, "rating_margin": 5.06, "sigma_race": 6.0, "sigma_total": 6.9, "baseline_margin": 5.29, "analyst_adjustment": -0.09, "key_factors": ["Nearly all expert raters call the seat a Democratic flip, from Tilt D to Likely D", "Prediction markets price Bennett around 82-83% to win", "Incumbent Kean faces union defections and reports of Republican concern about his chances", "Bennett has closed the fundraising gap and drawn outside support while Kean holds a cash edge"], "rationale": "The fundamentals point to a Democratic-favorable environment, and the sole recent poll plus the rater consensus both put Bennett up about five points. Markets at roughly 82% for Bennett align with a Lean D verdict, tempered by Kean's incumbency and cash advantage.", "watch": "A late shift in turnout or a Kean rebound among swing voters in the district's suburban towns could tighten the race.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Thomas Kean Jr. (R) is running."]}, "overview": "New Jersey's 7th District is a genuine battleground where first-term incumbent Thomas Kean Jr. is fighting to hold a seat with an even partisan baseline. Challenger Rebecca Bennett has raised over $4 million and attracted outside spending, while union groups including firefighters and AFSCME have backed her over the incumbent. Local reporting notes anxiety among some New Jersey Republicans about Kean's prospects.\n\nExpert raters overwhelmingly call the race a Democratic flip, ranging from Tilt D to Likely D, and prediction markets price Bennett's chances in the low 80s. The one recent live-caller poll shows Bennett ahead by five among likely voters. Kean retains a substantial cash-on-hand edge, keeping the contest competitive but tilted toward the challenger.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.8072, "margin": 5.29}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.8017, "margin": 5.2}]}